1.Apply the manifests:

Deploy all resources to your cluster in order:

kubectl apply -f 01-config.yaml
kubectl apply -f 02-redis.yaml
kubectl apply -f 03-frontend.yaml

2.Monitor the rollout

Check pod status and confirm all pods enter the Running state:

kubectl get pods -n mini-project-1 -w

Verify the services have internal cluster IPs:

kubectl get svc -n mini-project-1

3.Forward the frontend port to localhost:

Establish a port-forward tunnel from your workstation to the frontend service:

kubectl port-forward svc/frontend-svc 8080:80 -n mini-project-1

Open http://localhost:8080 in your browser. Each refresh increments the cache counter stored inside Redis.

4.Test resilience and zero downtime:

Run a continuous curl command in one terminal, then trigger a rolling restart of the frontend deployment:

kubectl rollout restart deployment/frontend-deployment -n mini-project-1
kubectl rollout status deployment/frontend-deployment -n mini-project-1

Because maxUnavailable: 0 is set with readiness probes, all requests continue without dropped connections.


